# Batch files check out with CRLF on every machine.
#
# Why, honestly: NOT because cmd.exe breaks without it. That was the reason I first
# wrote here, and I tested it rather than assert it -- an LF-only .bat containing a
# parenthesised multi-line block and `exit /b` ran identically to the CRLF one on
# Windows 11, same output, same exit code. Modern cmd.exe tolerates LF fine.
#
# The real reason is that without this the repo produces a DIFFERENT working tree
# depending on who clones it. There is no .gitattributes, core.autocrlf is true on
# the author's machine, so the blobs are stored LF and handed back as CRLF here --
# while a clone with autocrlf=false gets LF. That already showed up as drift: four
# of the five launchers sat on disk as CRLF and one as LF, for no reason anybody
# chose. eol=crlf makes the checkout deterministic and matches what a .bat is
# conventionally expected to be.
#
# So: defensive and tidying, not a fix for an observed break. If that distinction
# ever stops being recorded, someone will "fix" a bug that was never there.
#
# Nothing else is constrained on purpose. Forcing an eol on the .ps1 files would
# rewrite thousands of lines for no benefit, and PowerShell does not care.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Same, for the same reason: a Windows Script Host file is a Windows file. The
# rule is kept although session-restore no longer ships one -- "Sessions GUI.vbs"
# was folded into Sessions.bat's own fallback branch when the tool went down to
# two entry points. A .vbs added anywhere in this repo still wants CRLF.
*.vbs text eol=crlf

# Screen fixtures are byte-exact console captures, not source. They are the input
# to the ask parser, and the parser reads columns -- so a byte git changes on the
# way in or out is a byte the test no longer proves anything about.
#
# Measured before writing this: every capture is CR=0 today, in the blob and on
# disk, and a forced CRLF round trip changed no verdict (labels are trimmed). So
# this is defensive, like the .bat rule above -- not a fix for an observed break.
# What it stops is the SAME drift that rule exists for: core.autocrlf is true on
# this machine, unspecified attributes let git auto-detect, and a clone elsewhere
# gets a different working tree than the one the fixtures were captured in.
#
# -text, not eol=lf, and the difference matters: eol=lf would still normalise, it
# would just normalise deterministically -- a future fixture capturing a real CR
# (say, to prove the parser survives one) would be silently stripped on commit.
# -text means git never touches these bytes at all. `diff` is added back so they
# still review as text instead of "Binary files differ".
tools/session-restore/tests/screens/*.txt -text diff
